Z||000007||dcaprio||10-28-2001||03:20 PM||||We have a winner. Oil sender. If there is no oil presure the sender tells the fuel pump to turn off. Thanks for all the reply's.||||reg||1
Z||000008||87GTZ34||10-28-2001||04:18 PM||||Well you are 1/2 right so I'll give you a 50% partial credit...  Contrary to popular belief, the oil sender unit/fuel pump mechanism is not to shut the engine down with a loss of oil pressure.  It is a back-up system to the main fuel pump relay circuit so no one has an accident when the relay fails in traffic.  So in your case, your relay (or the relay circuit) is bad and your oil sending unit went bad.  It is typical for high milage engines to not carry enough oil pressure to keep the idiot light out during slow idle.||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||10-28-2001||04:34 PM||||87gt is correct in my opinion. all circuits being ok, changing fp relay or oil press sw would have solved problem of no start. But a fp relay working correctly should reduce starting time, since it doesn't have to wait for oil press to exceed the 4 psi.||||reg||1
